course: dictionary
lesson: 7894
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. Rap is one of them. The words around it, its context, tell you which meaning is right. «The crowd cheered as the rapper began to rap.» A rapper performs music, so here rap means the music style.
explain: «There was a rap at the door, so Mum went to see who had come.» A rap at the door is a quick, sharp knock, not music. Ask: what would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense. A knock fits, and music does not.
explain: Some meanings are only found in set phrases. «He took the rap for his brother.» Take the rap means to take the blame, even when someone else did the wrong thing. Phrases like this are easiest to learn as a whole, not word by word.
fact: Rap can be a noun or a verb.
fact: As a noun, rap is a style of music: «Rap uses rhythm, rhyme and wordplay, so it is a kind of poetry.»
fact: As a noun, a rap is a quick, sharp knock: «a rap at the door».
fact: As a verb, to rap means to knock or hit quickly and sharply, or to perform rap music.
fact: «A rap on the knuckles» means a mild telling-off, and «take the rap» means to take the blame.
exercise: «Rap uses rhythm, rhyme and wordplay, so it is a kind of poetry.» What kind of thing is rap in this sentence? | A kind of wet weather | A type of bread | A kind of dog | *A kind of music, with rhythm and rhyme
exercise: «There was a rap at the door.» What does rap mean here? | A telling-off | *A quick, sharp knock | To cut hair | Music with a fast beat
exercise: «He took the rap for his brother.» What does take the rap mean? | *To take the blame | To win a prize | To knock loudly on a door | To play a song
test: «The rapper stood up and began to rap about her school day.» What does rap mean here? | *Perform music with fast spoken words over a beat | Knock on a wooden door | Cry loudly | Hit something softly with a pillow
test: «A rap on the knuckles» is best described as: | A kind of dance | A reward for good work | *A mild telling-off | A serious injury
exam: Sam says, «He got a rap on the knuckles, so he must have been badly hurt.» Is Sam right? | *Sam is wrong, a rap on the knuckles is a mild telling-off, not a real hit | Sam is wrong, it means he won a prize | Sam is right, it means a serious injury | Sam is right, it means he was knocked over
